answer":"harmful","confidence":90,"evidenceIds":["E12","E2"]},{"itemId":"I23","answer":"sufficient","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E8","E14","E15","E10"]}],"evidenceRequests":["Q3","Q2","Q1"],"summary":"The OTP decline is real (manual ride checks agree with AVL) and is not driven by ridership or dwell. It stems from two operational changes in April: layover cuts on 21 routes and Halvorsen/Bridgeport construction on 5 of those routes. Group C, with neither change, held steady. The 40% absence rise is largely a recording artifact from the ShiftLine rollout, which now logs partial-day absences. Full-day absences rose only 1.1%, and dispatch data show no change in missed trips or late pull-outs. The proposed attendance discipline would punish operators on a measurement change and likely worsen layover-driven early leaves. Preferred actions are to restore recovery time, set construction running times, and restate the absence data before any policy decision."}}